===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|smuckle||dialectal}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:from|smuckle<ety:bor<nl:smokkelen<unc>><nds:smuggeln<unc>>>|tree=1}}
From earlier {{m|en|smuckle}}, either from {{bor|en|nl|smokkelen||to smuggle}}, a frequentative form of {{der|en|dum|*smugen|smūken|to act secretly, be sneaky}}, from {{der|en|odt|*smugan}}, or from {{bor|en|nds-nl|-}} or {{bor|en|nds-de|smuggeln}}; all are from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*smeugan|t=to creep; slip through or into}}.

{{col-top|2|cognates and related terms}}
Cognate with {{cog|stq|smuggelje|t=to smuggle}}, {{cog|fy|smokkelje|t=to smuggle}}, {{cog|nds-de|smuggeln}}, {{m|nds-de|smuckeln|t=to move insidiously, smuggle}}, {{cog|de|schmuggeln|t=to smuggle}}, {{cog|da|smugle||to smuggle}}, {{cog|sv|smuggla||to smuggle}}. Related also to {{cog|is|smjúga||to creep, penetrate}}, {{cog|sv|smyga||to sneak, slip, crawl, lurk, steal}}, {{cog|de|schmiegen|t=to nestle, wrap, snuggle}}, {{cog|ang|smēogan}}, {{m|ang|smūgan|t=to creep, crawl, move gradually, penetrate}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsmʌɡəl/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-smuggle.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌɡəl|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{wp}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[import]] or [[export]], [[illicitly]] or by [[stealth]], without [[paying]] [[lawful]] [[customs]] [[charge]]s or [[duties]].
#: {{syn|en|run}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Lorry driver jailed for smuggling illegal immigrants into UK|work=cps.gov.uk|url=https://www.cps.gov.uk/north-east/news/lorry-driver-jailed-smuggling-illegal-immigrants-uk|location=London|publisher=Crown Prosecution Service|date=2018-07-20|accessdate=2018-07-20|passage=A lorry driver who '''smuggled''' illegal immigrants into the UK in the back of his trailer has been jailed.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190922190004/https://www.cps.gov.uk/north-east/news/lorry-driver-jailed-smuggling-illegal-immigrants-uk|archivedate=22 September 2019}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 June 24|author=Vitali Vitaliev|title=Life on the borderline|journal=RAIL|issue=1064|page=68|text='Smuggling was a way of life here, with no stigma attached,' David explained. His own granny used to '''smuggle''' out rabbit meat, tied to her body, and bring back such coveted Italian goods as chocolate, coffee and brooms. 'Brooms?' I asked him in disbelief. 'Yes, ordinary sorghum brooms, which were in short supply in Communist Yugoslavia, like many other basic household goods,' he said with a smile.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring in [[surreptitiously]].
#* '''22 March 2012''', Scott Tobias, AV Club ''The Hunger Games''[https://web.archive.org/web/20120324175255/http://www.avclub.com/articles/the-hunger-games,71293/]
#*: While Collins does include a love triangle, a coming-of-age story, and other YA-friendly elements in the mix, they serve as a Trojan horse to '''smuggle''' readers into a hopeless world where love becomes a stratagem and growing up is a matter of basic survival.
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[fondle]] or [[cuddle]]. <!-- alt form of snuggle? -->
# {{lb|en|slang}} To thrash or be thrashed by a bear's claws, or to swipe at or be swiped at by a person's arms in a bearlike manner.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=23 June 2026|work=BBC|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cx2j74xqy7jt#Report|author=Phil McNulty|title=England 0-0 Ghana|passage=England finally found some urgency in the closing stages, when O'Reilly held his head in his hands after hitting the woodwork, with Guehi similarly disappointed to see his header '''smuggled''' off the line.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261004092429/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cx2j74xqy7jt|archivedate=4 October 2026}}

